WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.580
<v Mike>Galaxy 5000, racing in the 51st century, the best racing in the universe.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:30.600
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:31.640
<v Hosts>I'm Mike.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:40.000
<v Hosts>And I'm Sean. Is this like a method of future proofing your game so that we can't prove that this doesn't happen?

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:52.780
<v Sean>Yeah, I'd say like, you know, giving yourself a good 4000 years, you know, maybe 3000 and change is a pretty good way to make sure no one can say you were wrong.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:01:00.380
<v Mike>Right. I mean, we've seen with Back to the Future that like, you know, even in 1985, it's not a good idea to say what happens in 2015.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.620
<v Sean>Like that's gonna happen soon.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.680
<v Mike>We're gonna, you know, like we're not that far away.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.800
<v Mike>And then it did pass and those things didn't happen.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:15.440
<v Sean>Not unless you knew that something pretty bad was gonna happen in 2012, which I think we all know came true.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.160
<v Mike>Right, right, right, right.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.760
<v Mike>So the Mayans tried this long shot thing.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.080
<v Mike>And even they, you know, had to experience that, you know, time will pass.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:31.760
<v Mike>And so theoretically, there is a world where people are listening to this very episode, Sean, in the year 5000.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.720
<v Mike>Is there something you want to say to them?

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:39.000
<v Sean>Um, I'm gonna say I'm gonna have a few questions.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:44.700
<v Sean>My first question is, did we ever get Half Life 3?

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:49.840
<v Sean>And the second would be, can I come?

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:51.820
<v Mike>That's amazing.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:02:02.240
<v Mike>The first question is kind of funny, though, because you have to imagine that if at any point, we got Half Life 3 before the year 5000, that like they also just went on a rip after that.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.600
<v Mike>And it was like, yeah, four, five, six, and seven are all coming out like within years of each other after Half Life 3.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.240
<v Mike>It's like, just took a really long time to figure out that third one.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.640
<v Mike>But now we are just busting through them.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.360
<v Mike>And so that's like a bizarre question to someone.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.760
<v Mike>Yeah. In the year 5000.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.360
<v Sean>Or if I were to ask, did the Bills ever win the Super Bowl?

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.840
<v Sean>And then they just say, like, actually, there was a civilization ending asteroid impact.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.880
<v Sean>So American football hasn't been played in 3000 years.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.860
<v Sean>And the Bills didn't win one before that.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.640
<v Sean>And that's just what I think is going to happen.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.880
<v Mike>I think that that's a full possibility.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:42.480
<v Mike>And unfortunately, they probably can't even understand our language in the year 5000.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.420
<v Mike>So we should really be saying, gleek gloop glabagoober.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.360
<v Mike>Oh, I like that better.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.400
<v Mike>Right. It is in the year 5000.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.660
<v Mike>They're not even like talking in the same kinds of ways.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.340
<v Sean>No, no, no. Consonants, vowels.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.420
<v Sean>That's not a thing anymore.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:05.520
<v Sean>Right. Exactly. The, you know, those noises when you go between worlds in this video game, Galaxy 5000.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.180
<v Mike>Yeah, that's the, that's their actual language.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.340
<v Mike>They're predicting that. Yeah.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:10.900
<v Mike>Right. They predicted that.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.080
<v Mike>That's their first prediction that they're the Nostradamus of video games.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.500
<v Mike>If you haven't picked up yet, Galaxy 5000 is, uh, it, not only does it take place in the

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:29.960
<v Mike>future, it takes place in the 51st century, uh, where armored turbo craft racing is the, uh, sport of choice for thrill seeking individuals.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:37.240
<v Mike>And, uh, you start in the minor leagues on Mercury and try to work your way through the solar system.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:48.460
<v Mike>Uh, I guess that means that Pluto is the big leagues, but, uh, I guess this is their first mistake unless Pluto continues to be become a planet once again, but Pluto is not a planet anymore.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:49.960
<v Mike>So shouldn't it be ended at Neptune?

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:57.800
<v Sean>Uh, I mean, I guess you can just have these things happen on any kind of like planet toyd.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:05.260
<v Sean>Um, maybe, maybe they have a Grand Prix on series that we're not aware of that just isn't in this game, you know?

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.300
<v Mike>And, you know, the game isn't selling a deep story.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.000
<v Mike>Uh, you know, I was joking about like the minor leagues and stuff like that.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:20.580
<v Mike>It's just selling a vibe, you know, space racing prize money to fix your ship and blowing up your competitors.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:44.960
<v Mike>So it is kind of like combat vehicle racing in a way that we haven't, I mean, we've had it, but like not in the, not in the way that this game is doing it, where it kind of, this is like if RC Pro-Am was like thrown into outer space, added some jumps and, uh, did a, maybe RC Pro-Am had digitized voices, but there is a digitized voice in this game, which is pretty cool.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.180
<v Mike>It's like, get out of here.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:05:01.360
<v Mike>Right. The most important thing that's going to make or break any racing game, but specifically one that takes the RC Pro-Am isometric approach and puts it in space is how it feels to drive one of these bad boys.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.160
<v Mike>Sean, is it fun to drive one of these spacecrafts?

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.280
<v Sean>I'm going to go out on a limb and say yes.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:17.120
<v Sean>Uh, I think that that basic vibe, that, that basic feel from RC Pro-Am is there.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.320
<v Sean>Uh, I just have a soft spot for isometric games in the first place.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:29.240
<v Sean>And even though this game doesn't have like a, like a, the typical feel of a racer, like there's no accelerate button.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.660
<v Sean>It's just the direction you push is the direction you go.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:42.700
<v Sean>Yeah. But it also like, you have to take into account that the direction you push is also going to feel an amount of, uh, momentum.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.380
<v Sean>So it's also sort of like you're nudging it in that direction instead of actually going in that direction.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.940
<v Sean>It's kind of complex when you think about it, but when you actually push the buttons, it's very intuitive.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.980
<v Sean>Uh, it's just a little, you gotta get the hang of it is all.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:07.560
<v Mike>Yeah. And it's not like this hard to read, uh, 3D depth that we've seen with other games that try to do 3D because it's the isometric and we're kind of used to it at this point.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.240
<v Mike>Uh, it is, it is kind of like easy to tell when they're going to be turns.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.080
<v Mike>Uh, they're not just like sudden, uh, you know, all of a sudden a blinker on your left saying, okay, you're going to start turning left now.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.120
<v Mike>And then it's like a hard turn.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.180
<v Mike>Like we've seen in mock rider and games like that.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:35.140
<v Mike>I feel like visually the course, even when they do get more complicated with actual like gaps and obstacles in the course themselves, it's still a readable thing that makes it easy to, uh, understand how to, how to drive these things.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.680
<v Mike>You know, if you do have to take it slow or, or bank a turn or something like that.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:47.300
<v Mike>The interesting part of course comes not just from the racing, but the combat aspect because it's four ships racing at once and you can shoot your rival ships.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.920
<v Mike>You can destroy track obstacles.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:52.040
<v Mike>You have to jump over certain obstacles or pits.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:58.000
<v Mike>Um, otherwise you might fall off the course or take too much damage, taking damage during the races.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:10.700
<v Mike>Even if you successfully finish the race means that, uh, you will have to use some of your winning money, uh, your prize money to heal your ship or else you run the risk of permanently damaging your ship.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:18.140
<v Mike>That, that combat aspect is something that we haven't seen mixed with, um, these other like RC pro am style racing games.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:27.440
<v Mike>Um, or, you know, we've seen on some of the ones that are trying to do like a simulation, we've seen some sort of, uh, buying parts and stuff like that.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.900
<v Mike>But this game takes it to, um, uh, maybe a more arcadey level.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.140
<v Sean>Yeah. Like you're not really upgrading anything.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:45.640
<v Sean>You're, you're more so getting pretty impressive, like cosmetic damage onto your rig that you see between each race.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:56.540
<v Sean>And then you can choose to repair yourself and it kind of just like, it kind of wipes over the damage and you just, you get a clean new ride.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:08.840
<v Sean>Um, I'm not really sure how it affects like your stats or speed or whatever, because I always just completely repaired whatever vehicle I had between races.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.000
<v Sean>I don't really know what happens if you've kind of let it pile up.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:25.200
<v Mike>Yeah. I got to imagine that, uh, not only will you eventually just get a game over if you, if your money can't repair your ship, uh, because it is the kind of thing where like your money is based on your placing.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.460
<v Mike>So you don't have to finish in first to move on.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:38.340
<v Mike>You just have to finish at all because the actual, um, the actual track itself is a part of the, of the pressure of the game.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:45.600
<v Mike>Um, there's a clock that is counting down from, I don't know if it's always the same, but it's like, you got like basically two and a half minutes.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:54.560
<v Mike>Um, and when that timer runs out, especially like at the last three seconds, like the course just kind of fades out.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.380
<v Mike>Like the space obviously remains now you're going to dark.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:58.860
<v Mike>It's just the whole course disappears.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.400
<v Mike>Yeah. Raises some questions about the designs of these courses in general.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:10.540
<v Mike>Like, are these just projections with some kind of solid state to them so that they could hard light that they could handle?

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:15.140
<v Mike>Yeah. Right. Uh, but, but once that time is up, like that's it.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:20.500
<v Mike>So if you didn't cross the finish line, you can cross the finish line, like red at the end and still count it as a, as moving on.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.320
<v Mike>But if you don't, it's, it's, you know, that's the end of, uh, the race.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:34.900
<v Mike>So you have to complete these, not only, uh, in a good time to get, uh, the completion of the course, but in a good place to get enough money to repair, uh, your ship as well.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.000
<v Sean>Yeah. And it is an interesting moment.

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:43.160
<v Sean>Like when you get to that point where it just starts to fade away, cause it is gradual.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.600
<v Sean>It happens over, uh, like the last five, six seconds.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.860
<v Sean>So it, I think it's more so they turn the lights off.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.140
<v Sean>I think the, I think the track is still there.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:10:03.860
<v Sean>Um, but yeah, you'd have to, it gets a little bit harder to actually finish, but I'm sure it's for some people created some pretty dramatic finishes.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.760
<v Sean>Um, how, how did you perform in the game?

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.100
<v Mike>Um, you know, at first I was performing pretty great.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:16.360
<v Mike>Uh, I didn't have that much trouble learning, um, the driving, uh, aspect.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:33.440
<v Mike>And especially, uh, because the game lets you, uh, you can take jumps in a way where like, if there is a, a curve, but the curve meets back up in a specific way, you could take a long enough jump that you just skipped the whole bend and go to the next part of the track.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.880
<v Mike>And at first the computers won't think to do stuff like that.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:47.520
<v Mike>And so it's very easy to kind of just like, uh, skip parts of the track shortcuts, if you will, but intentional, the game wants you to do that kind of stuff to, to place first, uh, early on.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:11:05.980
<v Mike>Once the, like amount of obstacles and the amount of turns, uh, in the game increased, which happens, uh, pretty early on when even, even as soon as you get to like, uh, Venus or Mars, uh, then it got a little trickier to finish in first, but not to like complete the course.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.640
<v Sean>Yeah. Yeah.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.560
<v Mike>So I do think the game is forgiving in, in that front.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:11.640
<v Mike>Yeah.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:13.780
<v Sean>I also think that I appreciate that.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:20.800
<v Sean>Not every, not every, uh, NPC knows all of the shortcuts.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:24.420
<v Sean>Like they definitely do have them programmed in.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:34.780
<v Sean>So they're just going to do them and you might learn of them by watching them do it, but you also might be the first to find them, uh, in your race.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:37.060
<v Sean>And sometimes they'll go the long way around.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.620
<v Sean>So it's not like you're facing off against optimization opponents here.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.860
<v Mike>And you don't want the money just to repair your current ship.

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:53.560
<v Mike>You want the money. So this way you can buy, uh, the other ships in the game and, uh, upgrade, uh, the ships appropriately.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.440
<v Mike>There are five different models.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:00.460
<v Mike>There's the Tomahawk, the Crusher, the Phantom, the Scorpion, and the Stiletto.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:11.480
<v Mike>Uh, so naturally you don't want to just be using all your money to repair damage from the ship, which I guess if you played recklessly enough, you could just have like a lot of damage at the end of each one.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:19.360
<v Mike>Again, I don't think, especially in the earlier stages, that that would be something that would happen to you unless you were truly just careless.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:25.660
<v Mike>Yeah. Uh, but you know, as you want to progress and continue to get these better ships, uh, you'll, you'll need to do so.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:28.200
<v Sean>And they got pretty cool designs too.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:33.940
<v Sean>I mean, the naming convention is pretty, you know, there's a lot to be desired there.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:36.100
<v Mike>I feel like you don't want to fly in the crusher.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:43.540
<v Sean>I feel like crusher is, is something that you come up with when you're eight and then you just don't really think about it again.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:50.380
<v Sean>And you have to say it like crusher that, that and Scorpion, which I think like, you know, Scorpion is cool.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.560
<v Sean>Scorpion was moral combat.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:53.580
<v Mike>Yeah. You know what I'm going to say.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:58.740
<v Sean>But like, these are default cool middle school boy names.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:00.560
<v Mike>Right. Right.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:12.540
<v Sean>But the designs of the ships themselves are, uh, are pretty cool in terms of like what, uh, I mean, they're not like aerodynamic because you're, you know, you're in space and that doesn't matter.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:18.880
<v Sean>Do you think that's also why there aren't like cool noises for accelerating?

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.660
<v Mike>Like. Like there's no sound in space.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:23.420
<v Sean>I know there's no sound in space.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:30.440
<v Mike>I know. I know. I'm, I'm just coming up with probably the answer they came with when they were about to ship the game.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:33.960
<v Mike>And, uh, I think Activision was the publisher.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:35.180
<v Mike>Oh, they were also the developers.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.260
<v Mike>So it was like, they're talking to each other and they're like, shouldn't there be some more sounds?

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:40.360
<v Sean>No, it's fine.

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:43.980
<v Sean>We're being scientifically accurate here.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:52.040
<v Sean>Uh, but that's like the only thing that I found very like weird about the, like the, the, the feel of the races.

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:55.280
<v Sean>Like when I was thinking of, does this have a sense of speed?

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:57.880
<v Sean>I felt like, yes, it does.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:01.740
<v Sean>But the sound design is not doing anything for that.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:08.200
<v Sean>It's just in the handling and visually moving the screen fast.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:12.640
<v Mike>There are two, uh, control schemes too, which I thought was a little strange.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:19.220
<v Mike>And did you try the, the alpha controls are the ones that first off alpha controls, of course, I'm not a beta.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:25.240
<v Mike>I'm not gonna, I'm not gonna deal with that, but like the alpha controls, it's like pretty normal stuff.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:31.580
<v Mike>You know, you use the D pad to steer in the directions that the, that you need to steer the whole game in.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:32.500
<v Sean>And functionality. Yeah.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:37.600
<v Mike>Right. And the beta controls, all of a sudden I was like, what does it want me to do here?

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:43.440
<v Mike>Like I'm using up to accelerate and down to slow down the vehicle.

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:45.400
<v Mike>Like that's how I'm, I'm dealing my speed.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:46.460
<v Mike>But then I have to use this.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:47.640
<v Mike>It's a D pad people.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:48.880
<v Mike>It's not an analog stick.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.560
<v Sean>It's not too analog. Yeah, but it's like, it's tank controls.

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:53.180
<v Sean>That's how we played RC Pro-Am.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:54.800
<v Mike>That's how, really?

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:56.580
<v Sean>I'm almost positive.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.520
<v Mike>No, I know. I'm saying like, I didn't go back and revisit it.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:04.460
<v Mike>So it just felt strange after coming, because I started with the alpha controls to then go to the beta controls.

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.280
<v Mike>I was like, eh, I don't know about this.

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:07.800
<v Mike>I wasn't a fan of it.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:27.100
<v Sean>Yeah. I mean, I preferred, uh, the, I think that if it didn't have the kind of like the weight, if, if the characters of the characters, if the vehicles didn't have the kind of weight that they do, I would have preferred tank controls, but they are kind of weighty.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:32.240
<v Sean>So you're always, you're always kind of like trying to micro, micro steer.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:38.320
<v Sean>So I didn't really think the tank controls were necessary, but maybe some people, that's the only way that they can think.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:48.840
<v Mike>Well, the manual goes as far as to say that the beta controls are harder to get the hang of, but once you master them, they'll help you place better in the later runs.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.920
<v Sean>Yeah. That tracks.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:52.040
<v Sean>That makes sense.

00:15:52.240 --> 00:16:04.880
<v Sean>I mean, I would imagine you have a lot more, uh, you have a lot more like acute control over the angle at which you are driving as compared to the isometric viewpoint.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:16.460
<v Mike>Um, it reminds me of like any game, like any Gran Turismo where like when you finally go to manual mode instead of automatic transmission and it's like, Oh, like I, I can do a lot better.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:23.680
<v Mike>Like even, even the licenses in Gran Turismo are like designed to be really hard if you're using automatic transmission.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:26.660
<v Mike>Cause I don't think you can get gold on some of them.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.380
<v Sean>Is it even driving if you're using automatic?

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.300
<v Mike>That's so European of you.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:34.200
<v Sean>Yeah. I've never learned how to drive stick.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:35.580
<v Mike>So. Yeah.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:48.740
<v Mike>It's funny because I, I did learn how to drive manual for Gran Turismo games, but I still would never want to put myself in a car with like, I feel like something you need to learn inside and outside of a video game.

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:49.740
<v Mike>It's not, it doesn't apply.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.000
